# Writing

Write normal sentences. That is the whole voice. Explain the way a person in San Francisco would explain it out loud: someone who already knows some AI, wants context, does not like jargon, and does not like sentences that sound generated.

If you would not say it that way, it does not ship. Do not add another rule to dodge a bad line. Rewrite the line in normal English.

This site explains a chain of ideas. It is not an op-ed, a jobs essay, or a pep talk.

A new comment is a new constraint. Keep the old ones. Re-read this file before rewriting a paragraph. Do not throw out named examples because the last note was about jobs, or throw out jobs because the last note was about explaining.

## The reader

They live in San Francisco. They use ChatGPT, Copilot, and Cursor. They came here for context, not a glossary and not a TED talk. If they would have to google a word (Haar, rectifier, SWE-bench, wrapper), unpack it or skip it. If they would roll their eyes at a sentence, cut it.

## Normal sentences

Full sentences. Subject, verb, object. One thought after another. Set a scene if you need one ("Imagine you are playing basketball."), then write what happens in ordinary English.

Do not write:

- Chopped commands: "Hold out questions it never practiced on." "You check whether..."
- You-x-you-y lists: "You try a shot, you miss, you feel how far off it was, and then you nudge."
- Telegraphic beats: "Hide a word, guess it, and nudge."
- Half sentences and punch lines: "Sounds sure." "The install fails." "That is the transformer." "They need time."
- Fake mini-scenes for a general point: Jira, an org chart, old cameras, "ask ChatGPT about your company." If a general model has not been trained on your data, say that. Those guesses are hallucinations.
- Flipped metaphors: "the hole in reverse."
- Forced contrast: "It is X. It is Y." "a target, not a label."
- Lab words dropped to sound informed. Named people, products, lawsuits, and papers are fine.

A picture can name an idea once. "That pile of examples is data." After that, say data, examples, training. Repeating pile, pad, door, or knobs sounds like talking down.

Questions have to name the object. "Who decides what goes into the training data?" not "Who decides what counts?"

If the point is that rules did not scale, say that, then Fei-Fei Li. Do not swap in a camera demo.

## Page shape

Every topic page is the same:

1. Introduce the thing. Subject first, or a person in a scene, then the name. The Data gold sample is the model.
2. Then examples: a named lab, a year, a product doing a thing. OpenAI and Anthropic by name when that is the news. No model catalog SKUs.
3. Then two or three questions an intelligent person would actually ask. Answers add. They do not recap the intro.

Do not write a high-school picture or a college lecture. Do not ship a jobs question unless the answer is specific enough to survive a reread. Do not open a topic page on social impact, cheaper work, or whether people should be excited or afraid. Do not rewrite a page from scratch to satisfy only the latest comment.

The first two sections should sound like a New York Times explainer: complete sentences, a picture, then how the thing works. The third section should sound like The Information: a person, a year, a number. WIRED is the curiosity, not the punchlines.

## How an opening is allowed to start

Two shapes. Both explain. Neither lectures.

1. Subject first, then what people have used the word for.

   > AGI is a term that has been used by people for a machine that can do many kinds of intellectual work at the level of a person, without being rebuilt for each new job.

2. A person in a scene, then the name. The Data gold sample.

   > You don't teach a kid what a dog is by reading them a definition. You point at one, then another, then a cat, and after enough pointing they just know. A machine needs the same thing, only more of it. That pile of examples is data.

Do not hide the name at the end. Do not use "You don't..." as a costume for a definition. Do not open on mood or labor. After the point is made, stop. Do not write a leftover sentence that restates it in a vaguer phrase ("that work is no longer only theirs").

## Work, when it actually comes up

This site still has to tell the truth about work. It does not lead with it.

- Do not put a jobs FAQ on the AGI page until the answer is specific.
- Do not write "the first pass," "will not get fired," or "checking shrinks first."
- Do not sand replacement into "hard" or "a helper that shares the day."

## Before it ships

Read it out loud. Would a person say this? If not, rewrite it in normal sentences. Then:

- Ordinary verb (`used`, `do`, `ask`, `measure`, `score`), not `named`, `stay with`, `sit with`.
- People do things. Terms do not (`AGI named`).
- Delete the last sentence. If the paragraph still holds, it was leftover.
- Kill X-not-Y. Kill a hinge that starts by saying what the last thing cannot do.
- Casual metaphor already used? Switch to the ordinary word.

Every sentence has to happen because of the one before it. If you can shuffle them, it is notes. Before a paragraph ships, say its point in one clause.

## Gold sample

> You don't teach a kid what a dog is by reading them a definition. You point at one, then another, then a cat, and after enough pointing they just know. A machine needs the same thing, only more of it. That pile of examples is data.

A person does something. Then you name it. Then you say why. The why is about the idea, not about society.

> In 2009 a Stanford group led by Fei-Fei Li released a huge labeled photo set, ImageNet. The work was people looking at a picture and typing what they saw. In 2012 a deep stack trained on that set won the year's recognition contest by a wide margin.

A person, a year, a thing that happened.

## Numbers

When a page cites a live exam or a lab clock, look it up in the current month. Today is September 2026. Do not leave a 2025 launch score as if it were still true.

Prefer the official board: Humanity's Last Exam on Scale and the Nature paper; ARC Prize for Chollet's puzzles; METR for how long a software job can be. Round so the number can be heard, and keep the fact an engineer would not dispute.

As of September 2026, use these unless a fresh fetch says otherwise:

- Humanity's Last Exam: 2,500 expert questions, Nature in January 2026. Launch scores in 2025 were in the single digits. By September 2026 the best score on Scale's official board is about 55 percent, and some independent reruns sit a few points higher. The paper still says a high score is expert answers on closed questions, not a life you can hand over.
- ARC Prize, third version, March 2026: people solved every little game; machines scored under 1 percent. By September 2026 the score depends on the setup. One lab's system scored about 63 percent when it could only keep notes it chose to write down, and about 100 percent when the setup kept its private scratch work between turns. The puzzles stayed as hard as they were in March; the difference was the setup. The foundation declined to call the result AGI.
- METR: hours of well-specified software work a system finishes half the time, versus eight times out of ten. Some 50-percent measurements now bump the 16-hour ceiling of the suite. METR warns that measurements above 16 hours are not trustworthy on the current tasks, and that the suite is mostly clean programming work, not a whole job.

Do not paste model catalog names (letter-number SKUs) into the body. Proper names of people, labs, papers, and exams are fine. Unpack GPU, API, LLM, GPT, RLHF, and other letter soup.

Do not close AGI in eval-methodology voice ("staffing," "the suite," "bump the ceiling"). The last paragraph still has to be about the claim the word named, with the number attached. AGI is a word people use. It does not name, ask, or want.
